<p>I was sitting at my desk at home, pottering on the computer, when a grey shape flickered in my peripheral vision in the garden outside. This was quickly followed by a clattering of wings and a high pitched squawk. I looked out the window and quickly spotted him standing on his prey. There, at the centre of a blast radius of feathers, a male sparrowhawk.</p>
<p>We&rsquo;ve had sparrowhawks in the garden before, but they tend to be swift, fleeting visitors. A sparrowhawk&rsquo;s natural territory is on the edge of vision until it is too late. They are experts in low-level, twisting flight through cover. They suddenly, silently appear over a hedge or fence line, grabbing their prey on the way, then disappear just as quickly. In the literal blink of an eye, or between the beats of your heart, it is there and gone again, leaving only the intangible glamour of a patch of air that recently contained a sparrowhawk.</p>
<p>Often you only know that you have just missed seeing a sparrowhawk by the belated alarm calls of other birds, and a sad puff of feathers on the ground. In this case a nearby band of magpies on the roof of our neighbours&rsquo; shed set to their clattering calls, joining the higher voices of house sparrows. This time the sparrowhawk had won a bigger prize than one of the local sparrows, and stayed on the ground, behind our dogwood bushes, to pluck and eat it.</p>
<p>I rushed to fetch the binoculars, convinced he would have left by the time I returned. But no, there he was &mdash; a shifting slate and terracotta shadow behind the dogwood stems and leaves, revealing his magnificent bright yellow eye as he raised his head to watch for danger. The magpies were obviously interested in getting his prey, but they stayed back, squabbling and fighting with each other in a way that reminded me of a gang of would-be playground bullies watching the kind of small but deceptively tough kid who is surrounded by a atmosphere of danger. Magpies will take on many other kinds of predators, especially when in a group, but &mdash; even for me &mdash; sparrowhawks embody a serious message. I am sudden death.</p>
<p>The natural world is not sentimental, but humans can be. Even as I watched this bird intently through the binoculars, seeing the feathers float around as he plucked the prey and his bill turn glossy with blood, I found myself hoping that the bird he was feeding on was not one of the pair of collared doves who have recently been spending time in our garden. I then felt immediately guilty that I was trying retrospectively to do a deal with the fates to save a bird that <em>I</em> had decided was more worthy of life. As it turned out, it was a wood pigeon fledgeling who had a visit from The Death of Birds on this occasion.</p>
<p>It felt surreal to be witnessing this wildness in a tiny urban garden, like seeing a leopard casually drop over your garden fence and drink from your birdbath. Time stretched in this intense bubble of watching until &mdash; in the blink of my eye and between the beats of my heart &mdash; it was gone. All I caught was the edge of a slate shadow in my peripheral vision, slipping between the hawthorn trees.</p>